\section{Results: Phase 0 Pilot}

\subsection{Spectral Trajectory Characterization}
\label{sec:spectral_trajectory}

The Phase~0 pure-synthetic pilot (seed 42, $r_t = 0\;\forall\, t$) reveals a three-stage spectral trajectory across Generations~0--3: anisotropy decompression, decompression peak, and collapse onset.  Figure~\ref{fig:spectral_trajectory} visualizes this progression.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/spectral_trajectory.pdf}
\caption{Spectral trajectory across recursive generations (Pythia-410M). (a) Effective rank peaks at Gen~2 then declines. (b) Log-determinant shows larger relative decline. (c) Perplexity increases monotonically. (d) Lexical diversity (distinct-4) decreases monotonically. Dashed line: Gen~1 baseline (D004).}
\label{fig:spectral_trajectory}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Stage 1: Anisotropy Decompression (Gen~0$\to$1).}
The pretrained Pythia-410M checkpoint exhibits high representational anisotropy: the leading singular value $\sigma_1 = 1942.8$ dominates the spectrum, yielding a compressed effective rank of $\rho = 417.55$.  Fine-tuning on synthetic data redistributes variance across dimensions, reducing $\sigma_1$ to 804.0 (a 58.6\% decrease) and raising the effective rank to 491.77 (+17.8\%).  The log-determinant proxy increases correspondingly from 2366.68 to 2464.76, confirming that the representational volume expands as anisotropy dissipates.  This transition reflects a standard fine-tuning effect: adaptation to any downstream corpus disperses the pretrained model's dominant singular directions~\cite{biderman2023pythia}.  Crucially, this is not model collapse but rather a structural reorganization of the representation space.  For this reason, we designate Generation~1 as the baseline for all subsequent collapse measurements, excluding the pretrained-to-fine-tuned shift from the analysis.

\paragraph{Stage 2: Decompression Peak (Gen~1$\to$2).}
The spectral decompression continues into Generation~2 but at a sharply reduced rate.  Effective rank reaches its maximum at 496.44 (+0.9\% from Gen~1), and the log-determinant peaks at 2469.93.  The leading singular value continues to decline from 804.0 to 712.0, indicating ongoing but decelerating spectral equalization.  The near-zero growth rate signals that the decompression process is exhausting itself: the representation has reached its maximum uniformity under recursive synthetic training.

\paragraph{Stage 3: Collapse Onset (Gen~2$\to$3).}
Both spectral indicators reverse direction at Generation~3.  The effective rank drops to 493.31 ($-0.6\%$ from peak), and the log-determinant falls to 2440.57 ($-1.2\%$ from peak).  The asymmetry between these two declines is informative: log-determinant decreases at twice the rate of effective rank because it accumulates contributions from the full tail of the singular spectrum, whereas effective rank (spectral entropy) is dominated by the top singular values.  This differential sensitivity supports the use of log-determinant as the primary early-warning signal in our closed-loop controller (\S3.3), since it captures tail-spectrum degradation at greater magnitude than the effective rank ($-1.2\%$ vs $-0.6\%$).  Preliminary results from the 1B pilot suggest that the log-determinant may also lead temporally: at Generation 2, the log-determinant has already begun declining while the effective rank remains at its peak.  Full-scale confirmation is pending.  The simultaneous reversal of both metrics at the same generation boundary is consistent with a genuine inflection point; confirmation with additional seeds across the full 10-generation trajectory follows in Phase 1.

