\subsection{Collapse Indicators Cross-Validation}
\label{sec:cross_validation}

Table~\ref{tab:phase0_metrics} summarizes all tracked metrics for Generations~0--3.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Phase~0 pilot metrics across Generations~0--3 (pure synthetic, seed 42). Spectral metrics are computed on 1{,}000 probe samples; functional metrics on 5{,}000 held-out sequences. Percentage changes are computed relative to the Generation~1 baseline.}
\label{tab:phase0_metrics}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Gen~0} & \textbf{Gen~1} & \textbf{Gen~2} & \textbf{Gen~3} \\
\midrule
Eff.\ Rank ($\rho$) & 417.55 & 491.77 & \textbf{496.44} & 493.31 \\
Log-Det ($\mathcal{D}$) & 2366.68 & 2464.76 & \textbf{2469.93} & 2440.57 \\
$\sigma_1$ & 1942.8 & 804.0 & 712.0 & 700.68 \\
Perplexity ($\downarrow$) & \textbf{9.55} & 11.38 & 13.58 & 16.25 \\
Distinct-4 ($\uparrow$) & \textbf{0.7414} & 0.6256 & 0.5154 & 0.4900 \\
HellaSwag ($\uparrow$) & \textbf{0.4130} & 0.3897 & 0.3764 & 0.3658 \\
Train Loss & --- & 1.6228 & 1.2682 & 1.1340 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
% TODO: update with full Gen 0-10 data
\end{table}

The functional metrics paint a consistent picture of progressive degradation from the very first recursive generation.  Perplexity rises monotonically from 11.38 at Generation~1 to 16.25 at Generation~3, a 42.8\% increase that reflects the model's growing inability to predict real-world text distributions.  Distinct-4 declines from 0.6256 to 0.4900 ($-21.7\%$), indicating that the model's output vocabulary contracts as it trains on its own increasingly homogeneous samples.  HellaSwag accuracy drops from 0.3897 to 0.3658 ($-6.1\%$ from Gen~1), which confirms that commonsense reasoning erodes alongside lexical diversity.

The training loss trajectory provides a complementary signal.  Loss decreases from 1.6228 (Gen~1) to 1.1340 (Gen~3), a pattern that might superficially suggest improved learning.  In fact, this reflects the opposite: as the synthetic training distribution loses diversity across generations, the model fits it with less effort.  Lower training loss in this context is a symptom of distributional narrowing rather than improved generalization.

A critical temporal asymmetry emerges from comparing the spectral and functional indicators.  All three functional metrics (perplexity, Distinct-4, HellaSwag) begin deteriorating immediately at Generation~1, exhibiting monotonic decline from the first recursive iteration.  The spectral indicators, by contrast, continue to improve through Generation~2 before reversing at Generation~3.  The spectral inflection point thus lags the functional degradation onset by one generation.  This delay does not diminish the utility of spectral early-warning signals; rather, it highlights their complementary role.  Spectral metrics offer three practical advantages over functional indicators: (1)~they require no labeled evaluation set or external benchmark, (2)~they can be computed from a single forward pass during training without additional generation, and (3)~they provide a geometric interpretation of the collapse mechanism (loss of representational volume) rather than merely measuring downstream symptoms.  These properties make spectral signals suitable for closed-loop control, where evaluation cost and latency are critical constraints.

